   "file": "modules/delivery/server/release-tracking.ts",
   "fp": "265660f7eb4a",
   "in_delta": true,
   "line": 100,
   "message": "`serves()` writes a second copy of the rule that maps an environment to the category it completes. It decides with JS `String(e.name).toLowerCase() === c` and `is_prod`, while `categoryEnv()` (line 20) decides in SQL with `lower(name) = category` and `is_prod`. The strand guard and the completion lookup are the same rule, so build both from one source: have `strandsReleases` check `categoryEnv` for each category before and after the change, or derive `categoryEnv`'s matching from `serves`.",
   "original_severity": "minor",
   "pass": "review",
   "pre_existing": false,
   "rule": "B4",
   "scenario": "Today both copies agree on plain ASCII names. If either copy changes on its own (for example, a 'Staging' alias for UAT added to one of them), the guard lets a rename strand an open release, or blocks one it shouldn't.",
   "severity": "minor",
   "unclassified": false
  },
  {
   "class": "other",
   "file": "modules/delivery/module.ts",
   "fp": "68f2f997deee",
   "in_delta": true,
   "line": 122,
   "message": "`delivery.release-off-track` fires on every change to `overdue`, including `create` and `update`. It has no `except`, and the `ReleaseStatusChanged` payload has no `by`, so a PM who sets a target that has already passed gets an urgent \"is now Off track\" notice about their own write. The other owner rules skip the actor's own action with `except: \"by\"`. Add `by` to the payload and `except: \"by\"` to the rule.",
   "original_severity": "minor",
   "pass": "review",
   "pre_existing": false,
   "rule": "B12",
   "scenario": "Maya, PM of CON, creates CON v9.7.0 with a target of 2026-09-30 while the clock is pinned to Oct 16. The release starts as overdue, and Maya's bell gets an urgent \"Late release is now Off track\" about the release she just created.",
   "severity": "minor",
   "unclassified": false
  }
 ],
